# CatchFn canonical WatchFn delivery checkpoint

2026-10-20 UTC. CatchFn now accepts `watch: { endpoint, apiKey, ...transportOptions }` and constructs the shared `@watchfn/browser` client with automatic RUM disabled. Explicit bug capture bypasses sampling and DOM availability, preserving the canonical envelope. Existing injected capture clients remain supported. The endpoint path defaults to 64 KiB and rejects configured limits above WatchFn's per-event ingestion ceiling. Larger attachments require upload references.

Capture awaits shared authenticated delivery. Transient statuses/network failures use the existing bounded retry mechanism; authorization failures reject without automatic retry. Failed reports remain queued for explicit `flush()` recovery. Retrying `captureBug()` creates another report, so use the prepared delivery error’s `retry()` method (or `flush()` for an already queued report). See `browser-delivery-readiness.md` for widget recovery and actual browser evidence. Delivery is at least once; a successful insert followed by a lost response produces duplicate arrivals with the same report ID. There is no deduplication claim.

The CatchFn branch includes the WatchFn prerequisite and its current shared DB prerequisite; the CatchFn PR is stacked on the WatchFn branch so those changes are not duplicated in its diff. WatchFn validates canonical bug reports before ingestion and persists the unchanged envelope in its normal datastore. The separate flat-report CatchFn service remains available but is not the storage path used by this transport.

Validation:

- CatchFn SDK build/declarations and typecheck pass. All 83 SDK tests pass, including endpoint/authentication mapping without a DOM and payload ceiling validation.
- `scripts/watchfn-acceptance.mjs` exercises built CatchFn and WatchFn through actual loopback HTTP, bearer authentication and shared Kysely/SQLite persistence. It validates transient retry with an unchanged body, canonical report/query fields, final-hook redaction, nonretryable401, lost response duplicates, close/reopen persistence and dropped-table503 failure. Temporary servers, files and connections are removed.
- The native command used Node20.20.0 and `CATCHFN_WATCH_CORE` pointing to the built WatchFn prerequisite checkout. WatchFn was rebuilt after merging the latest shared DB prerequisites; its147 coretests passed. CatchFn's local full WatchFn build initially lacked optional framework adapter dependencies; the explicit built-prerequisite path avoids implying those framework builds passed in the CatchFn checkout. Browser transport and CatchFn builds ran in the CatchFn checkout itself.
- Native acceptance requires built shared DB, Kysely and a working better-sqlite3 binding. Build WatchFn in a checkout with its framework adapter prerequisites, or set `CATCHFN_WATCH_CORE` to that build's `index.mjs`. Run `node catchfn/typescript/scripts/watchfn-acceptance.mjs` from the repository root. Generated artifacts are excluded; no ignore changes are needed.

At this checkpoint, deduplication/atomic ingestion, application indexes, retention overrides and BotFn issue linking remained open. Subsequent [BotFn delivery](botfn-delivery-readiness.md), [issue-link polling](issue-link-readiness.md) and [canonical indexing](event-index-readiness.md) checkpoints supersede those corresponding gaps. Retention overrides, attachment channel provisioning, unload delivery/persistent offline recovery, performance/accessibility and deployment remain open. The original specification explicitly defers a canonical Python SDK; the separate Python flat-server package is not canonical transport parity. This is partial progress, not full readiness or a release.

`CatchFnConfig.integration` supplies an optional default `{target?, project?}` hint; `captureBug({integration})` overrides it per report. The event and canonical envelope retain that hint. The server chooses only a configured app/target/project route. Client options reject unsupported target values, empty/oversized projects, arbitrary extra fields and provider credentials. Provider keys and clients belong only in WatchFn server configuration. Server linkage fields remain supported on returned reports from an injected client, but are not accepted as creation hints.

A 202 acknowledgment means the canonical event and outbox entry are committed, not that a provider issue exists. Delivery is explicit server work. Exact replay under the enabled outbox deduplicates by namespace/app/report ID and immutable content; modified content conflicts. Original retry closures retain the ID/envelope. Without this opt-in Watch integration, existing generic arrival semantics remain at least once.

The Watch API key authorizes both ingestion and query and every route in its deployment. This is one trusted application boundary, not per-app/tenant authorization. Use an authenticated app-backend proxy if the full Watch key is unsuitable for browser exposure. Outbox namespaces do not make Watch queries tenant-isolated. Provider credentials stay server-side. Privileged delivery/status/reconciliation methods require host/operator access control if exposed remotely.

The TypeScript browser SDK is the canonical client described by this original specification. Existing Python code is the separate flat-report server contract, not a canonical Watch transport SDK; it remains unchanged and does not gain or claim BotFn delivery from this change.

## Validation

- CatchFn TypeScript SDK: 92 tests across 9 files pass, including default/override hint preservation, no false issue acknowledgment and malformed/credential-hint rejection. Source typecheck and dual-format/declaration build pass.
- `node catchfn/typescript/scripts/botfn-acceptance.mjs` passes using the built Catch SDK, actual shared Watch browser transport, built Watch core, real loopback HTTP, actual native SQLite and the actual BotFn GitHub client bundled from source. An ordinary PKCS#1 RSA PEM produces a verified JWT for an installation-token request before issue creation.
- The same acceptance launches installed Chrome and submits through the actual browser CatchFn SDK into Watch HTTP. It verifies pending acknowledgment without an invented link, authenticated routing, server delivery and persisted provider linkage.
- A dropped intake response triggers identical canonical replay and stores exactly one event/outbox. Pending state survives database close/reopen before delivery. Both Node and browser reports retain linkage across another close/reopen. Wrong API keys and unconfigured repository hints fail; redacted context persists without being included in provider issue text. Reprocessing linked rows sends no additional creates.
- Existing canonical HTTP/SQLite acceptance and actual Chrome cross-origin delivery acceptance pass unchanged with the integration disabled, retaining documented at-least-once semantics. The separate TypeScript flat-report server's four contract tests also pass; it remains outside issue delivery.
- Watch's separate native acceptance covers both actual GitHub and Linear request clients, two-connection claims, transaction rollback, lost provider response and verified reconciliation after reopening. See `watchfn/.conduct/botfn-issue-readiness.md` for exact scope.

Reproduction requires built shared DB, Watch browser/core and Catch SDK plus local `better-sqlite3`, `kysely`, esbuild, Playwright and Chrome. The acceptance script supports `CATCHFN_WATCH_CORE=/absolute/path/to/dist/index.mjs`. This run used the built Watch artifact from the identical merged Watch commit in the sibling readiness checkout: the Catch tree's intentionally scoped install lacks optional HTTP-adapter packages required by the full Watch multi-entry build. Catch's own SDK builds/types pass; the full Watch build and fresh packed matrix were validated in its own checkout. No dependency or lockfile changes were needed.

## Remaining original gates

No hosted issue or message was created. Local protocol fixtures exercise real clients and authentication code, not live GitHub installation permissions, Linear workspace access or provider reliability. The original hosted-provider acceptance, per-tenant query/auth design, scalable event query indexing, production worker/retention operations, BotFn package deployment and v1.1 closed-issue synchronization remain open.

The later issue-link checkpoint adds an authenticated status route and opt-in bounded reporter polling; see `issue-link-readiness.md`. The application still owns proxy authorization and worker scheduling. Linear's current request interface cannot cancel the underlying operation and both existing provider clients parse full response JSON; Watch's wait timeout is not a universal transport/memory bound. Unknown provider outcomes are parked for verified operator reconciliation and are never automatically recreated. This is a tested canonical integration checkpoint, not full CatchFn/WatchFn/BotFn readiness.
# Asynchronous verified issue links

This closes the reporter's asynchronous issue-link display gap through WatchFn's authenticated read-only status route. It does not change standalone `captureBug()` acknowledgment or schedule provider writes in the browser.

Enable `issueStatus.enabled` to poll after accepted reporter submissions. Default bounds are 10 reads, 1-second spacing and a 15-second total deadline; validated limits allow 1–30 attempts, 100–10000ms spacing and 10–60000ms total duration. Linked or reconciliation states stop immediately; attempt exhaustion returns the latest status/null. HTTP/parser failures, cancellation and deadlines reject without resubmitting or marking an accepted report unsent. Reporter close, replacement and Catch stop cancel active reads/delays. A provider that ignores AbortSignal cannot hold the polling promise past the total deadline or update the UI later.

Endpoint clients use BrowserClient's actual status reader and derive `/mount/issues/status` from `/mount/ingest`. Ambiguous paths require explicit `watch.issueStatusEndpoint`. The same full Watch API key authorizes status, ingestion and query; this is not a new credential scope. An injected `issueStatus.read` or Watch-client `readBugIssueStatus` can call an authorized application backend so the browser need not possess that key. Backend authorization remains the application's responsibility. Both network and injected receipts must bind the requested app/report and coherent state/link. Only expected-provider HTTPS GitHub/Linear issue URLs without credentials/query/fragment are rendered; unverified or dangerous links never become DOM hrefs. Immediate injected-client issue receipts receive the same link checks.

The UI keeps accepted reports accepted while showing waiting, unavailable, operator-attention or verified-link state. It does not offer delivery retry on a status error. `pollIssueStatus` is also available programmatically; standalone capture never calls it automatically.

Validation includes eleven focused SDK tests for independent acknowledgment, attempts/terminal states, deadline and stop with a signal-ignoring provider, ignored late receipts, forged identity/URLs, provider mutation of expected identity, actual endpoint/header reuse and configuration limits. The native BotFn acceptance now uses the mounted `/watch` route and actual Chrome reporter: it observes pending state, runs the real server worker, then sees the verified provider link. HTTP503 and a dangerous fake receipt preserve acceptance without links or resubmission. Closing during a hanging status request cancels polling; all six accepted reports remain unique, are delivered by the server worker, and retain linkage through SQLite close/reopen. Actual BotFn GitHub authentication and issue requests still go only to a local protocol fixture.

The separate Python flat-report service remains unchanged; it is not the canonical browser client. Hosted provider permissions, worker deployment, retention, tenant-specific query/auth design, provider transport/memory limits and v1.1 issue-close synchronization remain open. Bounded status reads do not remove the existing BotFn client's unbounded provider-response parsing or Linear cancellation limitations.

Full SDK suite: 103 tests across 10 files; source typecheck and dual runtime/declaration builds pass. Fresh browser/DrawFn/CatchFn tarballs pass ESM and CommonJS status/polling runtime checks plus strict Node16/NodeNext public declaration emission, including React props sharing the root CatchFn class. This revealed and corrected CatchFn's preexisting conditional-export mismatch: require consumers now resolve the generated `.d.cts` declarations instead of ESM `.d.ts`; runtime entry points remain unchanged. No dependency or lockfile changes were needed.
# Browser discard integration

2026-10-20. Catch incorporates Watch commit `0526b7b6234ae79371a5fe9c3df45db839ab1bcf` by ordinary merge `ed1cd24527f95a2ac7856c9ee76cc2823cd1d577` onto Catch `259419bc5`. The integration adds the underlying browser transport discard API and generation fencing without changing Catch capture, retry, widget, retention, or synthetic/alert behavior. Catch does not expose a new consent API in this change.

Validation on the integrated tree:

- Rebuilt `@watchfn/browser` CJS/ESM/IIFE/declarations before rebuilding Catch.
- All103 Catch SDK tests pass, plus SDK/admin TypeScript checks and Catch CJS/ESM/declaration builds.
- `botfn-acceptance.mjs`: built SDK and Chrome through actual Watch HTTP/native SQLite, close/reopen, actual BotFn GitHub client against local HTTP fixture, replay deduplication, auth/route denial, verified widget links, bounded payload expiration and retained replay/status receipts pass.
- `watchfn-acceptance.mjs`: actual HTTP/native SQLite reopen, auth, canonical metadata, authenticated query, storage failure and retry pass.
- `browser-delivery-acceptance.mjs`: actual Chrome cross-origin delivery, same-ID503 retry, offline/CORS recovery, auth/redaction, lost-response at-least-once arrivals and explicit new-report choice pass.

The browser dependency is built from this integrated checkout. `CATCHFN_WATCH_CORE` points to the built sibling Watch core at identical source; the merge changes browser code only. All commands exited0 and acceptance finally handlers closed browser/server/database resources and removed their temporary files. No external provider issue or deployment was created.

Logs: `/tmp/catch-discard-{tests,browser-build,build,types,botfn-acceptance,watchfn-acceptance,browser-delivery-acceptance}.log`. Run existing commands from `query-streaming-integration-readiness.md` after rebuilding the browser dependency. The discard primitive itself is validated by39 Watch browser tests and actualChrome localHTTP in `watchfn/.conduct/browser-discard-readiness.md`.

These are local integration receipts, not hosted readiness or arbitrary remote retraction. Discard cannot retract a request already handed to the network. Existing at-least-once delivery, Watch API-key authority and original outstanding readiness gates remain unchanged.
